WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.712
In this guided practice the goal is to find
diagnoses that appear

00:00:03.738 --> 00:00:08.500
in more than one admission Start with the grain We
want one

00:00:08.525 --> 00:00:13.712
output row per diagnosis so diagnosis belongs in
GROUP BY Next choose

00:00:13.738 --> 00:00:18.075
the measure We want the number of admissions for
each diagnosis so

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:22.462
COUNT star is appropriate Now think about the
condition more than one

00:00:22.488 --> 00:00:27.738
admission That condition describes a completed group
count not an individual source

00:00:27.762 --> 00:00:33.062
row Therefore it belongs in HAVING not WHERE The
query groups by

00:00:33.088 --> 00:00:37.100
diagnosis applies HAVING COUNT star greater than one
and then sorts the

00:00:37.112 --> 00:00:42.762
remaining diagnoses by admission_count descending
Run the query and inspect which

00:00:42.775 --> 00:00:48.350
diagnoses survive A diagnosis that appears exactly
once should not be included

00:00:49.238 --> 00:00:52.538
This exercise is a good example of how the five-step
workflow works

00:00:52.562 --> 00:00:56.462
in practice define the question set the grain choose
the measure place

00:00:56.488 --> 00:01:00.850
the group filter correctly and validate the result
If you can explain

00:01:00.862 --> 00:01:03.788
why HAVING is used here instead of WHERE you
understand one of

00:01:03.812 --> 00:01:06.212
the most important ideas in aggregate SQL
